# Automated PR Review Gate (Pre-Merge)

Before merging any PR to `main`, **trigger and fully resolve all automated review feedback** on that PR.

**Workflow detail:** follow the correction loop in `@.cursor/rules/013-pr-review-correction-loop.mdc` (CI quiescence, all bots, thread reply/resolve, rate-limit patience).

## Bots / sources in scope

Address findings from **all** of these when present:

- **CodeRabbit** (`coderabbitai`) — **always** obtain a real review on the latest head before merge (not a rate-limit placeholder), **except** under the bounded fallback in the rate-limit loop below (step 6): after 3 failed wait/re-trigger cycles, or a single wait exceeding 90 minutes, with no real CodeRabbit review yet posted for the current head, Sourcery may stand in.
- **CodeAnt** / Code Antai (`codeant-ai`)
- **Sourcery** (`sourcery-ai`) — comment `@sourcery-ai review` to trigger/re-trigger on the latest head. Address inline `suggestion`/`nitpick`/`suggestion (bug_risk)` comments the same as other bots — `bug_risk` findings are correctness issues, not style nits, and get the same fix-first treatment as CodeRabbit/CodeAnt findings.
- **GitHub Copilot** (`copilot-pull-request-reviewer`)
- **DeepSource** (`deepsource-io`) — **always** comment `@deepsourcebot review` after opening a PR and after every push that needs re-review (team AI Review is on-demand; static analysis alone is not enough). Prefer a top-level comment whose first line is exactly `@deepsourcebot review`. If the PR report still shows the on-demand notice / no `DeepSource: AI Review` check appears, a human collaborator may need to re-post (DeepSource can ignore GitHub App bot mentions). Also address Docker/Shell advisory findings when actionable. Dashboard JavaScript analyzer remains off (`docs/project-facts.json` / `docs/deepsource-javascript-ci.md`); ESLint + `deploy.yml` stay authoritative for TS/TSX.
- Other automated PR reviewers that post inline or summary comments (e.g. Socket Security actionable items when they block merge, Cursor Bugbot when requested)

Human review comments that request concrete fixes are also in scope when they are open on the PR.

## Scope (mandatory)

Address **every** item, including:

- Critical / high / medium findings
- **Nitpicks**
- **Outside-of-diff-range** comments
- Suggestions, refactors, and doc notes from the reviewer

## Workflow (loop until clear)

1. After pushing PR updates, **trigger on-demand reviewers**: `@coderabbitai review` when a full review is missing/stale/rate-limited, `@sourcery-ai review` when Sourcery hasn't reviewed the latest head yet, and **always** `@deepsourcebot review` (DeepSource AI Review is on-demand for this team — static analysis alone does not count as a completed AI Review).
2. Collect **all** review threads (inline + summary + out-of-range) from every in-scope bot, including DeepSource AI Review findings.
3. For each item: fix in code/docs, or reply with a clear English rationale if intentionally deferred (rare — prefer fixing).
4. Push follow-up commits; re-trigger `@deepsourcebot review` and CodeRabbit as needed; **repeat until no new actionable comments remain**.
5. Do **not** merge while unresolved automated review comments remain.

## CodeRabbit rate-limit loop (mandatory — never skip)

A green CodeRabbit check that only says **“Review rate limited”** is **not** a completed review. Do **not** merge on that alone.

1. Parse the PR summary / comment for **`Next review available in: N minutes`** (or similar).
2. **Wait** at least `N` minutes (add ~2–5 minutes buffer). Prefer sleeping/polling over guessing; typical windows are **14–60+ minutes**.
3. Re-poll: issue comments + `pulls/N/reviews` for `coderabbitai[bot]` on the **current** head SHA.
4. When the window has elapsed, post a clean top-level `@coderabbitai review` (and `@coderabbitai summary` only if needed). Prefer re-trigger over relying on auto-review after a rate-limit event.
5. If still rate-limited, read the new wait time and **repeat** up to **3** wait/re-trigger cycles on the same head (document each attempt in a PR comment).
6. **After 3 failed wait/re-trigger cycles, or a single wait exceeding 90 minutes, with no real CodeRabbit review yet posted for the current head** — this exact threshold, repeated verbatim in `013` and `docs/ci-branch-governance.md` — CodeRabbit becomes **optional** for that PR rather than a hard block, **provided** Sourcery has posted a real review (not rate-limited) on the current head with all actionable findings resolved, and no other in-scope bot has an unresolved actionable finding or an active `CHANGES_REQUESTED`. Post a disposition comment documenting the rate-limit timestamps/cycles and that Sourcery is standing in as the primary review for this PR. This is a standing policy (maintainer-approved 2026-08-05), not a one-off exception — no need to re-request permission on future PRs that hit this same threshold. This fallback applies **only** while CodeRabbit has produced no real review of the current head; once a real review lands — including `CHANGES_REQUESTED` — the fallback no longer applies (see step 7).
7. **Single exact quiescence predicate** (this is the one authoritative rule — `013` and `docs/ci-branch-governance.md` restate it, they do not vary it): quiescence requires **either (a)** a real, **non-`CHANGES_REQUESTED`** CodeRabbit review (`APPROVED` or `COMMENTED`, with a review body proving it isn't a rate-limit placeholder) targeting the **latest** head commit, **or (b)** the optional-CodeRabbit fallback in step 6 — **and, in both cases, all actionable threads are resolved**. A `CHANGES_REQUESTED` review **never** satisfies quiescence by itself, no matter how detailed its body — that state means the bot is actively requesting changes, not clearing the PR. After fixing a `CHANGES_REQUESTED` review's findings, re-trigger CodeRabbit and wait for a fresh `APPROVED`/`COMMENTED` review on the new head; a `CHANGES_REQUESTED` review is only moot once **superseded** by such a fresh review on a later commit (not resolved by thread-resolution alone).

## Exceptions

- Duplicate comments already fixed in a later commit: mark resolved and cite the commit.
- False positives: short English reply explaining why + resolve thread after confirmation.

See also English content policy `@.cursor/rules/010-english-content.mdc`.

<example>

CodeRabbit shows "Review rate limited" on the current head. Parse the wait time, wait it out (+buffer), re-trigger `@coderabbitai review`, and repeat up to 3 cycles / 90 minutes. A real `APPROVED`/`COMMENTED` review lands on cycle 2 — all inline threads it raised are fixed and resolved — then merge.

</example>

<example type="invalid">

CodeRabbit shows "Review rate limited" once. Merge immediately without waiting, re-triggering, or checking whether Sourcery has a real review with everything resolved (step 6's fallback conditions were never met — no wait/re-trigger cycles were attempted at all).

</example>
